From: Angelo Arifi <arifilabs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 01:01:37 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] docs: USAGE - a task-oriented guide for humans, not an option
 reference

The existing docs are mechanism-oriented: one page per switch, with the evidence attached.
That is the right shape for an engineer auditing a claim and the wrong shape for someone who
just wants to run a model. OPTIONS-REGISTRY has a single table cell of ~15,000 characters;
it is a reference and should stay one.

USAGE.md is organised by intent - 'run a ternary model', 'no GPU', 'model bigger than RAM',
'speculative decoding' - each with the command, the reason, and the measured outcome including
where the answer is 'turn it off'. It also states plainly which capabilities exist here that
stock llama.cpp does not have, and credits the source project for each.

Origin: ArifiLabs lane-110D, public-release bar
Measured-effect: documentation only; every figure quoted is copied from OPTIONS-REGISTRY or
committed trial evidence, none newly measured.
