+# Using this fork
+
+Start here. This page is organised by **what you are trying to do**, not by what options exist.
+If you want the full reference — every switch, its default, and the measurement behind that
+default — that is [`OPTIONS-REGISTRY.md`](OPTIONS-REGISTRY.md), and it is deliberately exhaustive
+rather than readable. This page is the readable one.
+
+Three things worth knowing before you start:
+
+- **Everything this fork adds is off unless you turn it on.** Drop in a model and it behaves like
+  stock llama.cpp, with one exception: ternary 128-group models load, which upstream cannot do.
+- **Every default was chosen by a measurement on one machine** (an AMD Radeon 780M mini PC). At
+  least one of those defaults is provably wrong for a CPU-only machine. Where that matters, this
+  page says so.
+- **The build flags matter more than usual on Windows.** If you have not built yet, read
+  [`BUILDING.md`](BUILDING.md) first — three flags there are not optional, and skipping them
+  produces a link error or a binary that dies at startup.
+
+---
+
+## What this fork can do that stock llama.cpp cannot
+
+| You want to… | Possible with stock? | Here |
+|---|---|---|
+| Run a **ternary model** (2-bit, 128-value groups — the "Bonsai" family) | No — the file is refused at load | Works, no flag needed |
+| Run a **mixture-of-experts model larger than your RAM**, streaming experts from disk | No | Yes, see [Bigger than RAM](#bigger-than-ram) |
+| Get **fast ternary/1-bit maths on a CPU-only machine** | No | Yes, one environment variable |
+| Read **AMD ROCmFP4/FPX** quantized files | No | Yes, rebuild with one CMake flag |
+| Read **TurboQuant TQ3_1S/TQ4_1S** files | No | Yes, rebuild with one CMake flag |
+| Run **q4_0 / iq4_nl / mxfp4 with CPU repacking on Windows** | Crashes (upstream bug) | Fixed here |
+
+---
+
+## Recipe 1 — a ternary model (Bonsai and friends)
+
+Nothing special required. The model loads because this fork understands the 128-value-group
+layout that upstream does not.
+
+```bash
+llama-server -m Ternary-Bonsai-27B-Q2_0.gguf -ngl 99 -c 4096
+```
+
+A 27-billion-parameter ternary model is about 7 GB on disk, so it fits on machines that could
+never hold a 27B in any other format. On the reference machine it generates at 12.5–13.3 tokens
+per second; the 8B version reaches 36.8.
+
+**If the model fails to load with a tensor-offset error**, you have a file whose type tag does not
+match its actual layout. `tools/gguf/` contains a retagging utility that fails closed — it will
+refuse rather than guess.
+
+## Recipe 2 — you have no GPU (CPU-only machine)
+
+**Change the default.** This is the one case where the shipped default is measurably wrong:
+
+```bash
+GGML_ARIFI_VNNI_REPACK=1 llama-server -m your-ternary-model.gguf -dev none
+```
+
+On a CPU-only path this made prompt processing **327% to 442%** faster and generation **20% to
+24%** faster, on a machine with AVX-512. Ranges did not overlap across nine runs per arm.
+
+It is **off by default** because on a machine with a GPU the same feature makes prompt processing
+**77–88% slower** — repacked weights lose eligibility for batch offload to the GPU. One feature,
+opposite verdicts, so it ships off and the server prints an advisory when it detects the
+configuration where turning it on is clearly right.
+
+If your build has Vulkan compiled in but you are running on CPU, add `--no-host`, or the repacked
+path is never actually reached.
+
+## Recipe 3 — AMD integrated graphics / unified memory
+
+This is the reference configuration, so it is the best-tested path.
+
+```bash
+llama-server -m model.gguf -ngl 99 --n-cpu-moe 0 -c 4096 --ctx-checkpoints 0 -fa on -ub 512
+```
+
+- `-ngl 99 --n-cpu-moe 0` puts everything on the GPU via shared memory. When the model fits, this
+  is the fastest arrangement measured — 29.2 tokens/sec on a 35B mixture-of-experts model.
+- `-c 4096` is not cosmetic. At default context the cache does not fit and the model fails to
+  load; the context size is what makes it fit.
+- `--ctx-checkpoints 0` disables prompt rewind. Leave it off unless you need rewind — it was the
+  single largest speed win ever measured on this hardware.
+- Keep the KV cache at f16 unless you are short on memory. It measured speed-equal to the
+  quantized cache, so there is no reason to give up the precision.
+
+<a name="bigger-than-ram"></a>
+## Recipe 4 — a model bigger than your RAM
+
+For mixture-of-experts models, most weights sit idle on any given token, so they can stream from
+disk instead of living in memory.
+
+```bash
+EXPERT_BUNDLE_PATH=/path/to/bundle.bin \
+MAX_N_CACHED=20000 \
+llama-server -m model.gguf -ngl 0 -b 8 -ub 8
+```
+
+- `EXPERT_BUNDLE_PATH` points at a generated expert bundle. Without it, nothing streams.
+- `MAX_N_CACHED` bounds how many experts stay cached in RAM. Larger is faster until you run out
+  of memory; measure rather than guess.
+- `POWERINFER_IOCP=0` switches Windows back to simple blocking reads. It defaults to the
+  asynchronous path; if you hit a stall, this is the first thing to flip. **Honest note:** we
+  could not measure a speed difference between the two, because our test model's experts fit in
+  the OS file cache and there was little disk traffic to overlap. Correctness is proven; the
+  speed question is open.
+
+Expect single-digit tokens per second. Streaming trades speed for the ability to run the model
+at all.
+
+## Recipe 5 — speculative decoding (draft models / MTP)
+
+A small "draft" model guesses several tokens and the big model checks them in one pass. When it
+works it is a large win. **It is not universal** — sweep the depth, do not copy someone's number.
+
+```bash
+llama-server -m model.gguf --model-draft draft.gguf \
+  --spec-type draft-mtp --spec-draft-n-max 3 -fa on -ub 512 -c 4096
+```
+
+Measured outcomes on the reference machine, same mechanism:
+
+| Model | Without | With | Verdict |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Gemma-4-26B-A4B | 29.3 t/s | **37.3 t/s** at depth 3 | worth it |
+| A 31B with root MTP | 1.8 t/s | **9.4 t/s** | dramatic |
+| A Qwen mixture-of-experts | 29.2 t/s | 22.8 t/s tuned | **slower — turn it off** |
+
+That last row is why you sweep `--spec-draft-n-max` yourself.
+
+There is also a target-specific drafter for the ternary family
+(`--spec-type draft-dspark --spec-draft-n-max 4 -ngld 999`). It measured **0.54×** — a real loss —
+on the reference machine, so it is off. Note `4` is mandatory there; other depths crash.
+
+## Recipe 6 — hardware you do not want to tune by hand
+
+```bash
+llama-server -m model.gguf --arifi-profile cpu-only
+```
+
+Profiles set a coherent group of options and **print verbatim what they selected and why**. There
+is deliberately no automatic detection: whether a CPU-side optimisation helps depends on whether
+*these specific tensors* end up offloaded, which is decided after startup — so a startup probe
+cannot answer the question the default depends on. See [`HARDWARE-PROFILES.md`](HARDWARE-PROFILES.md).
+
+---
+
+## Extra formats — rebuild required
+
+These are build-time options, not environment variables, for a specific reason: the CPU maths for
+a quantization type is bound when the binary is linked, so a runtime switch could not cover it.
+
+```bash
+# AMD ROCmFP4 / ROCmFPX files
+cmake -S . -B build -DGGML_ARIFI_ROCMFPX_FORMATS=ON
+
+# TurboQuant TQ3_1S / TQ4_1S files
+cmake -S . -B build -DGGML_ARIFI_TURBO_WEIGHT_QUANTS=ON
+```
+
+Both default to off. With the option off, a file in one of those formats is **refused with a clear
+error**, never misread — that is deliberate.
+
+Fair warning on TurboQuant weights: on our CPU path `TQ4_1S` measured **438 MiB at 4.5 tokens/sec**
+against plain `Q4_0` at **409 MiB and 49.3 tokens/sec** — bigger and about eleven times slower,
+because no hand-optimised kernel exists for it yet. It is shipped for people on hardware where
+fused kernels do exist. We are not going to pretend that number is good.
+
